{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01F56VT84fuhzxKJhdnPf5C9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q8: RLHF pipeline: SFT, reward model, PPO. How does DPO simplify?**\n\n---\n\n**Key content in `04-008-rlhf-pipeline-sft-reward-model-ppo-how-does-dpo-simplify.md`:**\n\n**Three-stage RLHF pipeline with formulas:**\n1. **SFT** — standard cross-entropy on curated (prompt, completion) pairs; becomes the frozen reference for KL\n2. **Reward Model** — pairwise ranking loss `L_RM = -E[log σ(r_φ(x, y_w) − r_φ(x, y_l))]`; SFT backbone + scalar head; InstructGPT used 6B RM on 33K comparisons\n3. **PPO + KL leash** — `objective = E[r_φ(x,y)] − β·KL[π_θ || π_SFT]`; β=0.01–0.1; too small → reward hacking (verbosity/sycophancy); too large → no alignment gain\n\n**DPO math explained:** The optimal KL-constrained policy has a closed-form expression; substituting into the RM's Bradley-Terry loss cancels the partition function Z(x), yielding a log-probability-ratio binary cross-entropy — no RM, no PPO needed\n\n**RLHF vs DPO comparison table** with quality ceiling, training stability, compute cost, and when-to-use guidance\n\n**Real examples:** InstructGPT (1.3B outperforms 175B base, 85% win rate) vs Zephyr-7B (DPO on GPT-4-generated synthetic preferences, competitive with GPT-3.5)\n\n**3 pitfalls** including the most common miss: not stating the KL penalty and failing to derive *why* DPO works vs. just saying it's simpler"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_read_input_tokens":69963,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":404,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6057c0a6-16a6-4e15-a6a9-b329e7c7286b","uuid":"24bab2d5-cb11-4d87-b91c-990b66fa7601"}
